{% when 'integrations-mcp-guide' %}
<div class="tk-guide-hero"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">INTEGRATIONS & MCP</span><h1>Connecting systems without losing context</h1><p>Understand supported connector coverage, preserved identifiers, and governed AI extensions.</p><div class="tk-guide-meta"><span>TRACEKIT LEARNING LIBRARY</span><span>6–8 MIN READ</span><span>EDUCATIONAL GUIDE</span></div></div>
<div class="tk-guide-layout"><aside class="tk-guide-sidebar"><strong>IN THIS GUIDE</strong><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#chapter-1">01 / What a connection means</a><a href="#chapter-2">02 / Preserve provider evidence</a><a href="#chapter-3">03 / Verify readiness</a><a href="#chapter-4">04 / How MCP fits</a><a href="#chapter-5">05 / Plan your onboarding</a></nav><a href="/pages/resources">← All resources</a></aside><article class="tk-guide-article">
<section id="chapter-1"><span class="tk-guide-index">01 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>What a connection means</h2><p>An ecosystem listing does not guarantee a production-ready connector. Confirm each provider's supported events, access scope, sync behavior, and rollout stage.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Featured platform ≠ universally available connector"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Featured platform ≠ universally available connector</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-2"><span class="tk-guide-index">02 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Preserve provider evidence</h2><p>Keep original source IDs and raw provider values alongside normalized forms where supported. Distinct orders, refunds, rebills, and disputes should not be flattened.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Raw source → Normalized record → Relationships</p></div></section>
<section id="chapter-3"><span class="tk-guide-index">03 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Verify readiness</h2><p>Confirm authorization, supported topics, historical scope, sync freshness, failures, and unresolved mapping behavior. A successful login alone does not prove ingestion coverage.</p><div class="tk-guide-diagram"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONCEPT</span><div class="tk-guide-flow" role="img" aria-label="Authorize → Verify → Reconcile"><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 01</small><strong>Authorize</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 02</small><strong>Verify</strong></div><span class="tk-guide-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="tk-guide-flow-step"><small>STEP 03</small><strong>Reconcile</strong></div></div></div></section>
<section id="chapter-4"><span class="tk-guide-index">04 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>How MCP fits</h2><p>MCP enables compatible external assistants to discover supported tools. Authentication, client context, permissions, and production policy govern actual availability and execution.</p><blockquote>Tool discovery is not action approval.</blockquote></section>
<section id="chapter-5"><span class="tk-guide-index">05 / GUIDE SECTION</span><h2>Plan your onboarding</h2><p>List the questions you want answered, the sources holding relevant evidence, and the exact event types required. Test representative records and document gaps before expanding.</p><div class="tk-guide-insight"><strong>Key point</strong><p>Start with evidence coverage, not logo count.</p></div></section>
